<Project>

  <!--
    The collector's settings for a process that is a service node. Import this into the
    host's project file, or copy the four settings: they are startup configuration, so they
    belong to the executable and not to a library. docs/enrichment-service.md, "Sizing a
    node", has the measurements behind each (benchmarks/Incremental.Service.Scale, a
    million wide rows and a million orders of state, per node):

    Server, concurrent, not adaptive (DATAS off). A node holds gigabytes that stay and
    allocates steadily on top of them. Workstation collection spent 17 % of a leader's
    steady state paused and could not keep up with its inputs; the adaptive mode held the
    same memory as the fixed settings below and paused longer.

    Heap count 8, or the cores the node is given if that is fewer. Every heap has its own
    budget of memory to allocate into before a collection, so a heap per core on a large
    machine holds a great deal ready that twenty one-worker circuits never fill; four
    heaps held a little less than eight and paused three times as long. Set NodeGcHeaps
    before the import for another count.

    Not set: System.GC.ConserveMemory. At 5 and at 7 the leader's working set was 0.3
    to 0.4 GB lower in the steady state at a million rows (3.1 and 3.0 GB for 3.4) and
    no lower at five million. A backlog was worked through a sixth more slowly in some
    runs of every setting, and in more of them the more was conserved (one of seven,
    three of seven, seven of nine) — though the order the runs were made in may account
    for that. A setting that asks for full blocking collections is not taken on that
    evidence; a host that wants the memory adds
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.ConserveMemory" Value="7" />.

    Not set: System.GC.HeapHardLimit. It is how a container's limit is kept: see the
    guide for what a limit of 1.35 times what the node holds cost.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>0</GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>
    <NodeGcHeaps Condition="'$(NodeGcHeaps)' == ''">8</NodeGcHeaps>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.HeapCount" Value="$(NodeGcHeaps)" Trim="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
